The Chaperone authentication gateway project has successfully completed Phase 0 foundation infrastructure (8 sub-phases, 104 tests, 0 technical debt), Phase 0.9 CLI foundation (5 commands, 15 tests, working templates), Phase 1 basic proxy server (2 components, 20 tests, transparent HTTPS tunneling), and 36% of Phase 2 MITM and service routing (4 of 11 work items, 25 tests, service registry + domain matching + policy enforcement complete but NOT integrated with proxy server), providing a rock-solid foundation with excellent error handling, structured logging, configuration management, context propagation, graceful shutdown, working HTTPS proxy functionality, and complete service routing logic that can identify configured domains and enforce policies, but the project has implemented ZERO TLS interception capability, ZERO CA certificate generation, ZERO dynamic certificate generation, ZERO MITM integration with the proxy server (service routing exists but is unused), ZERO secret providers, and ZERO authentication strategies, resulting in a codebase that is approximately 22% complete relative to the PROJECT_SPEC.md specification requirements, with service routing logic fully implemented and tested but not wired into the proxy server, and the critical path blocked by the absence of a working MITM engine (CA generation, certificate generation, TLS handshake) and integration code that connects service routing to the proxy tunnel handler.

